Go's GC is concurrent and mostly runs alongside the program; stop-the-world pauses are typically well under a millisecond. Go 1.26 turned on the Green Tea GC by default, which scans memory by spans and cuts GC CPU cost further. The real latency cost is usually **GC assist and CPU share** under heavy allocation, not pauses.

How to keep tail latency stable:
- reduce allocations (preallocate, reuse buffers, `sync.Pool`, avoid needless pointers);
- tune `GOGC` and set `GOMEMLIMIT` to use available memory instead of collecting too often;
- make sure `GOMAXPROCS` matches the CPU quota (automatic since Go 1.25 in containers), otherwise CFS throttling causes much larger stalls than the GC;
- profile with pprof and `GODEBUG=gctrace=1`, and watch p99, not averages.

Pitfall: answering "no, because of the GC" (a common answer). Many latency-critical systems (trading gateways, databases, proxies) run on Go; only hard real-time with microsecond guarantees needs C/C++/Rust.

